{"name":"Grey Wolf","hex":"#9ca0a6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grey wolf","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large canid, Canis lupus, once found throughout forested areas of northern Europe, Asia and America; it shares a common ancestry with the domestic dog."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey_wolf"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grey wolf (mythology)","blocks":[{"text":"The Grey wolf or Graywolf is a sacred animal and national symbol in Turkic, Mongolian, and Altaic mythology. In Turkish it is known as Bozkurt. In Mongolian it is called Börteçine."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_wolf_(mythology)","wikidata":"Q4090231"},{"title":"Wolf","blocks":[{"text":"The wolf, also known as the grey wolf or gray wolf, is a canine native to Eurasia and North America. More than thirty subspecies of Canis lupus have been recognized, including the dog and dingo, though grey wolves, as popularly understood, include only naturally occurring wild subspecies. The wolf is the largest wild extant member of the family Canidae, and is further distinguished from other Canis species by its less pointed ears and muzzle, as well as a shorter torso and a longer tail. The wolf is nonetheless related closely enough to smaller Canis species, such as the coyote and the golden jackal, to produce fertile hybrids with them. The wolf's fur is usually mottled white, brown, grey, and black, although subspecies in the arctic region may be nearly all white."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Eurasian_wolf_2.jpg/330px-Eurasian_wolf_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolf","wikidata":"Q18498"},{"title":"Grey","blocks":[{"text":"Grey or gray is an intermediate color between black and white. It is a neutral or achromatic color, meaning that it has no chroma. It is the color of a rain or storm cloud, of ash, and of lead."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg/330px-Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey","wikidata":"Q42519"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/5_Turkish_Liras_%281927%29.jpg/500px-5_Turkish_Liras_%281927%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/5_Turkish_Liras_%281927%29.jpg/1280px-5_Turkish_Liras_%281927%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/5_Turkish_Liras_%281927%29.jpg/500px-5_Turkish_Liras_%281927%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"5 Turkish Lira banknote with the image of Bozkurt issued on December 5, 1927","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A5_Turkish_Liras_(1927).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Tianshan_tianchi.jpg/500px-Tianshan_tianchi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Tianshan_tianchi.jpg/500px-Tianshan_tianchi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tianchi Lake and Bogda Shan in the Mountains of God \n(Fukang, Changji Hui Autonomous Prefecture, Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATianshan_tianchi.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Tianchi_Lake.jpg/500px-Tianchi_Lake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Tianchi_Lake.jpg/1280px-Tianchi_Lake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"ht